  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: \n\nA series of mid level impulses tracking eastward along the \nnorthern periphery of an expansive 500mb ridge will interact \nwith anomalous moisture/instability and strong WNW flow aloft \nto promote a multiday severe storm and excessive rain risk \nacross portions of CPA Tuesday-Thursday. A surge of late summer\nheat (max temps 80-90F range) with dewpoints in upper 60s to low\n70s will result in a very moist boundary layer with impressive\nMLCAPE values in the neighborhood of 2-3k J/kg given mid level\nlapse rates around 7 C/km. Forecast soundings also show robust\ndeep layer/effective shear values and appreciably curved, \nsizable low level hodographs. As such, a couple of supercells \nwill be possible. Severe wind and hail are the main threats, \nthough a tornado or two may also occur. On balance, the \nenvironmental parameters are quite bullish for severe storm \npotential. However, timing (time of day), speed, and location \nof each mid level impulse will be key factors that could \nmodulate the bullish severe setup to some extent. Increasing \nMUCAPE values late tonight through Tuesday morning suggest \nstorms/severe risk likely won't be tied to the typical diurnal \ncycle. The severe risk will shift to the south of CPA by \nFriday, but an upper trough passage suggests some lingering \nshower activity into the weekend. \n\nThe late summer heat is forecast to peak Tuesday and Wednesday \nwith very warm nights +10-20F above the historical average and \nmax heat indices approaching advisory levels/century mark \nacross parts of south central PA. The upper ridge is forecast to\nretrograde by the end of the week which allows for increased \ntroughing and a return to more seasonal temperatures for early \nSeptember.",
